; =============================================================================
; Tunah tuner -- peephole optimization
; =============================================================================
; Tunah note    : A peephole here is a rewrite over a window of two adjacent
;                instructions in one basic block, matched with seq. Nothing in
;                this file looks further than the next instruction, and no rule
;                here reasons about a value that is already available, which is
;                what separates it from reduntant-instr-elim.tuner.
; Purpose       : Remove no-ops from a block, forward a store into the load
;                that reads it back, and delete a store that is overwritten
;                before anything can read it. No rule here changes a memory
;                effect: the forwarding rules keep the store and truncate the
;                forwarded value to the stored width, and only the
;                dead store rules remove one, and then only when the next
;                instruction rewrites the very same bytes.
; Stage         : Instruction level machine IL, on a linearised block.
; Vocabulary    : seq, nop, the stores and the loads of the shared directory
;                vocabulary.
; Preconditions :
;   * The model of memory is a flat array of 8-bit bytes, (storeN p v) writes
;     the low N bits of v little endian at the byte p, and (loadN p) reads
;     them back. Loads and stores are not speculatable and are not reordered
;     across the window, so the second instruction observes the first.
;   * A load or store that may trap, has an ordering constraint, or may alias
;     through a barrier must not be represented as loadN or storeN. The
;     adapter has to reject such instructions before ingestion, otherwise the
;     window reasoning below would not hold.
;   * A nop has no effect and is not observable, so dropping it is safe even
;     between two memory instructions, where a scheduling barrier would not
;     have been.
; Cost model    : No-op and dead-store removal reduce size. Narrow forwarding
;                needs a truncating mask and can grow the term; it wins only
;                when the configured load cost pays for that mask. Otherwise
;                extraction retains the original load.
; Termination   : pp-drop-nop-first and pp-drop-nop-second remove a node from
;                the window, pp-drop-double-nop removes a whole window, the
;                forwarding rules replace a load with a value or mask, and
;                the dead store rules collapse two instructions into one store.
;                No rule creates a nop, a store or a load, and forwarding never
;                recreates a matched load, so application remains bounded.
;
; Operator signatures required by this file:
; signatures: seq 2, nop 0, store8 2, store32 2, store64 2, load8 1, load32 1,
;             load64 1, iand 2
; =============================================================================

; A nop in the first window position is not an effect, so the window is its
; second instruction.
(rule pp-drop-nop-first (seq (nop) ?r) ?r)
; A nop in the second window position is not an effect, so the window is its
; first instruction.
(rule pp-drop-nop-second (seq ?l (nop)) ?l)
; The second instruction reads exactly what the first wrote, so the load is
; forwarded the stored value and the load disappears. The store is kept, not
; deleted: a later reader of that location may still need it, and this tuner
; has no liveness information, so the value is forwarded without the memory
; effect being touched. Valid only because the window is adjacent, so nothing
; can intervene between the store and the load. The load returns only the low
; 32 bits written, even if the stored value arrived in a wider register.
(rule pp-store-load-forward32 (seq (store32 ?p ?v) (load32 ?p))
  (seq (store32 ?p ?v) (iand ?v 4294967295)))
; Same, for the double width pair.
(rule pp-store-load-forward64 (seq (store64 ?p ?v) (load64 ?p)) (seq (store64 ?p ?v) ?v))
; The byte read back is the byte just written, so the load is forwarded the
; stored value, again without touching the store.
; Only the low byte is read back; for example, storing 256 then loading yields 0.
(rule pp-store-load-forward8 (seq (store8 ?p ?v) (load8 ?p))
  (seq (store8 ?p ?v) (iand ?v 255)))
; The first store is overwritten before anything can read it, because the very
; next instruction writes the same address, so it is dead.
(rule pp-dead-store32 (seq (store32 ?p ?v) (store32 ?p ?w)) (store32 ?p ?w))
; Same, for the double width pair.
(rule pp-dead-store64 (seq (store64 ?p ?v) (store64 ?p ?w)) (store64 ?p ?w))
; Two adjacent nops are one nop.
(rule pp-drop-double-nop (seq (nop) (nop)) (nop))
